From: Nirmal Unnikrishnan <71024015+nunnikri@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 16:43:55 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kpack): fall through to next matching kpack archive on
 kernel-not-found (#12518)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Temporary backport of https://github.com/ROCm/rocm-systems/pull/12518.
The pinned rocm-systems revision bc176d4b86e86cf5caf5dc44fc2beec442ad6e38
predates this fix. Remove this patch when the pin includes upstream commit
694068a0c1610c61e22ff33ef574849cd172f13d. Avoids a 130-commit submodule
bump for the rocBLAS gfx942:xnack+ / bare-gfx942 archive lookup failure in
https://github.com/ROCm/rocm-libraries/actions/runs/37138192117/job/111247271746.

## Motivation

JIRA ID: ROCM-31644

`kpack_load_code_object()` committed to the first archive whose
architecture tag matched the requesting agent's ISA, and returned
`KPACK_ERROR_KERNEL_NOT_FOUND` immediately if that archive didn't hold
the kernel — even when a less-specific but still ISA-compatible archive
existed and actually held it. A single architecture's kernels can be
split across multiple kpack archives (e.g. an `xnack`-tagged archive
holding a handful of kernels for one binary, plus a bare/generic archive
holding the rest for a different binary sharing the same kpack group),
so the loader needs to try every compatible candidate before giving up.

This revives [#9874](https://github.com/ROCm/rocm-systems/pull/9874),
which fixed this exact defect for
[ROCm/TheRock#7081](https://github.com/ROCm/TheRock/issues/7081) (torch
2.12 + ROCm 7.14, `hipErrorInvalidImage` on gfx90a) but was closed
without merging once that specific trigger was worked around in
PyTorch's own packaging rather than by fixing the loader.

The same underlying defect is independently hitting rocFFT-test on
gfx950 ASAN builds (see Issue Tracking). Root cause there is upstream in
rocFFT's own build: `projects/rocfft/CMakeLists.txt` unconditionally
strips all `GPU_TARGETS` feature flags before compiling (`list(TRANSFORM
GPU_TARGETS REPLACE ":[^;]+" "")`, comment: *"rocFFT builds generic code
for each target"*), so rocFFT-test's kernels always land in the bare,
untagged kpack archive — even on ASAN builds where TheRock's own
sanitizer configuration deliberately forces `gfx950` → `gfx950:xnack+`.
Meanwhile hipFFT-test, a separate project sharing the same `fft_test`
kpack group, has no such stripping logic and is correctly tagged.

## Technical Details

- `kpack_load_code_object()`: instead of stopping at the first archive
whose architecture tag matches, collect every `(archive,
matched_target)` candidate across the full compatible-target power set
for an architecture (most specific first), then try `kpack_get_kernel()`
against each in order until one succeeds.
- Every miss along the way is logged and recorded in `last_err`; if
every candidate is exhausted without success, the original error
(`KERNEL_NOT_FOUND`, or another error type) is still returned —
preserving the existing `ARCH_NOT_FOUND` vs `KERNEL_NOT_FOUND`
distinction for accurate diagnostics, unchanged from #9874's original
behavior.
- Rebased cleanly onto current `develop` (which already has #9953's
partial fix) with zero conflicts in the affected file.
- Added a second regression test alongside #9874's original 1-feature
case (`gfx90a:xnack-` → falls through to bare `gfx90a`): a 2-feature
(`sramecc+`/`xnack+`) scenario mirroring the rocFFT-test/gfx950 case
exactly, where two of the four power-set candidates have no archive at
all — not merely a kernel-not-found miss — to cover that the collection
loop correctly skips candidates with no matching archive on the way to
the one that has it.

## Test Plan

- Generated all four `.kpack` test fixtures via `generate_test_data.py`
(ran cleanly, verified output).
- Traced the fix logic by hand against the real archive layout pulled
from a gfx950 ASAN tarball (`kpack_get_binary`/`kpack_get_architecture`
listing) to confirm it resolves the actual ROCM-31644 failure.
- **Not yet done**: a real compiler/CTest pass of `test_kpack_runtime`
(including the pre-existing `LoadCodeObject_KernelNotFound` test, to
confirm no regression there).

